ge  usually you're gonna be seeing this if  he's thought through and that's exactly  what he has because it's so tough for  Bobby Fischer to take care and he really  can't and will go through the variations  if you were to take with a C pawn then  the pawn takes back here if the pawn  were to take then the Bishop takes and  you can see Bishop here on d5 attacking  this rook here on a8 can't really do  anything because it's blocked off by the  knight right there so Spassky doing a  good job of understanding that this  Knight is stopping this rook from  getting involved into the game now if we  come back and say what happens if the  other pawn takes so pawn from a six if  he takes okay pawn takes but still not  good  we know that the pond can't take right  here well okay we need to protect this  pond right here there's no good way to  do that there's no square that you can  really get to where that feels good even  if you have something like Queen to see  seven people try to hold on to this pond  we'll just punt up here to d6 that's not  gonna work for black he's gonna be  losing that material so this Queen here  on a5 although it came down to try to  get involved into the game really he  would like it back here to try to defend  so in the game after Knight takes on d5  Bobby Fischer decides to go completely  different route and just play Bishop to  g5 just say okay I recognized that I  made a mistake I gave up a pond of  material I'm gonna continue with my game  plan here so Bishop to g5 but you pup  here to h5 pawn takes now Bishop takes  on f7 so you see some crazy changes  going on from buddy Spassky saying yeah  you can't take with your king so I'm  okay if you want to take with your rook  here rook takes rook up here to f7 and  then Bobby Fischer says yeah let's go  ahead and get the Queen down here if you  want to exchange on board and that's  what they do this looks much more like  what Bobby Fischer wants exchange down  material he's no longer in a really  terrible position and he's getting more  towards the drawl state which he seems  to have been doing in the previous games  rook over here to f1 Knight to c6 now  opening up the door for his other rook  to get involved into the game pawn takes  on d5 pretty much have to take back with  the pawn rook to d7 Bishop up here to e3  and then King to h1 after the check from  Bobby Fischer and Spassky moving is King  to safety Bishop comes back here to d4  starting to gobble up some of those  pawns he does need to get back in this  game since his opponent has two rooks on  board pawn to e6 so white does have to  passed pawns he can start to push we see  black play Bishop to e5 if you look at  and say why doesn't he goblet material I  always have to be wary of your  opponent's rooks because we're over here  to see one as it is going to gobble up  one of these pieces on the board  even if rook to you  ates gobble up the bishop lose the pawn  buttons gonna lose the pawn here on d5  definitely going to be a good exchange  for Spassky so after pawn to e6 we see  Bishop to e5 rook takes on d5 rook over  here to e6 looking to gobble up material  rook to e1 attacking the bishop but the  bishop is being defended by this night  here on c6 so bishop or the rook can  easily come down to e6 take rook to d6  we see the King get involved into the  game would be a mistake if the bishop  were to take because Bishop takes and  then rook up here to e6 Bishop takes and  then the Knights gonna follow you can  see how bad this gets because this rook  is now attacking both of these minor  pieces if we come back and say what  happens if the rook takes okay well  that's gonna be bad as well pawn takes  here this is gonna be a dangerous pawn  to deal with and you see the same thing  if the bishop were to take then the rook  can just come up here to e6 now after  the pawn were to come here if we see the  King to f7 well then we see we're cover  here to c1 attacking the knight it just  gets tricky the rook can come up here to  c7 check start to gobble up some pawns  on the board so all in all do not want  to take that rook although it may look  tempting for Bobby Fischer to do so  decides instead it's better to get his  King involved into the game so King to  f7 rook takes on c6 rook takes and then  rook to e5 so we've come into the in  game with rooks apiece it does look like  White has an advantage but it's gonna be  fairly easy for Bobby Fischer to  actually gobble up a pawn back in  material and then both sides are really  equal there's no clear way for either  side to have an advantage we see Bobby  Fischer has the more advanced King so  he's gonna go ahead get that into the  action King here to f6  fortunately the rook to move King here  to e6 rook back to h5 and then pawn age  6 King to h2 rook to a6 so saying yeah  if I'm not gonna take this I can easily  take this pawn here on a2 there's no  real way for white to hold on to it so  he plays pawn to c6  as black decides to go ahead and take  this pawn here this allows passkey to  come over to a5 that's gonna be fine  pawn to a6 this blocks this pawn from  coming off the board but it does protect  and allows Spassky to hold on to his  pawn on the a file and this is just a  completely draw state they do have a  couple more moves King to g3 King f6  King f3 rook to c3 then after King to f2  and rook to c2 they decide to go ahead  and draw the game so after 40 moves we  do have another tie so game 19 at I felt  like Bobby Fischer this is exactly his  game playing right now especially with  the black pieces there's no reason to  give your opponent any hope that they're  gonna be coming back so hopefully you  guys enjoyed this video we do have a few  more games left and the best of 24  series Bobby Fischer has still a 3 point  lead but thanks for watching I'll see  you in the next one

